# How to read the metrics

## Pearson r vs Lin's Concordance Correlation Coefficient (CCC)

**Pearson r** measures co-variation: if two estimates rise and fall
together across samples, r ≈ 1, even when one is systematically
shifted (e.g. half the magnitude of the other). It answers "do the
two estimates rank samples the same way?"

**Lin's CCC** measures agreement around the y = x line. A systematic
bias — for example, one tool reporting consistently 10 percentage
points higher than another — drops CCC even when r is near 1. It
answers "are the two estimates *the same number*?"

The largest gap between r and CCC for a label is the single best
summary of calibration drift.

## MAE quantiles

**MAE** is the mean of the per-sample absolute differences in
proportion (units of 0–1). We report the mean (`mae_mean`), the
median (`mae_median`, robust to outliers), and the 95th percentile
(`mae_p95`, the tail).

## Jaccard@τ

For a threshold τ ∈ {0.10, 0.25, 0.50}, **Jaccard@τ** is the
intersection-over-union of the two estimates' "this sample has at
least τ proportion of ancestry X" sets. Robust to small per-sample
floating-point differences; sensitive to whether the two tools agree
on which samples cross a meaningful threshold.

## μ-gating

`cluster_mu` is the mean proportion of an ancestry across the
cluster's samples. When `cluster_mu < 0.01`, there is essentially no
signal for that ancestry in that cluster, and any metric computed
on it is degenerate. The report **gates** these pairs:

- they appear with `pass = null` (μ-gated, neither pass nor fail);
- they contribute to a `μ·∅` column in summary grids;
- they are excluded from aggregate "fraction passing" rolls-up.

## Pass criterion

A (cluster, label) pair **passes** when both of the following hold:

- `pearson_r ≥ 0.95`
- `ccc       ≥ 0.90`

Pairs with `cluster_mu < 0.01` are μ-gated (pass = null, not a
failure). Everything else is a fail.

## ARI / NMI / V-measure (when hard calls are available)

For sections that compare two tools' categorical (argmax) calls, we
also report three label-permutation-invariant cluster-evaluation
metrics from the standard literature: **Adjusted Rand Index**
(Hubert & Arabie), **Normalised Mutual Information**, and
**V-measure** (Rosenberg & Hirschberg). These do not depend on how
labels are matched between tools and so provide an honest floor
against which the mapped confusion matrix should be read.
